+# Flow exact-image click hook (v7.0.89)
+
+## Problem
+Flow starts image downloads by clicking a detached blob anchor (`a.click()` or a synthetic click).
+v7.0.88 logs show that a failing save follows the browser's own download event, and the
+Chrome crash log lists a crash within a second of each failing download event. The in-page
+capture never saw a click (`messages=0`), because detached anchors do not reach document listeners.
+
+## What changed
+- `engine/flow_ui_blob.py`: while armed, `HTMLAnchorElement.prototype.click` and
+  `dispatchEvent` are hooked. A click on an exact blob anchor (same origin, `download`
+  attribute) is taken in the page: `fetch` resolves the blob at click time, so revoking it right
+  after the click does not matter. The bytes are sent in chunks through the existing binding, and
+  Python validates the file before it is moved into place. No browser download starts.
+- If the in-page read fails, or Python rejects the bytes, the original click runs (the browser
+  download), as in earlier releases. The fallback runs only while the capture is still open.
+- The hook is removed when the capture closes; the browser's original click behaviour is restored.
+- `engine/flow_download_diagnostics.py`: the Playwright `Browser logs:` tail (the browser's own
+  stderr, last 10 lines, capped, URLs removed) is kept in failure text, instead of being dropped.
+- `engine/providers/flow_browser.py`: the launch transport and whether `DISPLAY`/`WAYLAND_DISPLAY`
+  are set are logged once after launch (`browser launched: ...`), so a crash can be matched to it.
+
+## What it does not do
+- It does not change which page is clicked, which tile is chosen, or whether a new image is generated.
+- It does not reproduce the Chrome crash. The crash cause is still unproven. Compare the next
+  runtime log's `browser launched` line and any `Browser logs (last lines)` tail with `chrome://crashes`.
+
+## Verification
+- `tests/test_v789_blob_click_hook.py` (9 tests): unit tests for the log tail and the hook script,
+  and real-browser tests through Playwright Python. The browser tests use Chromium
+  (set `AUTOVIDEO_TEST_CHROMIUM`) and skip if none can be launched. Result in the build sandbox:
+  Chromium 131 (npm `@sparticuz/chromium@131`), 9 passed.
+- Standalone checks in Chromium 131 with the production script: `a.click()` with an immediate
+  revoke, synthetic `dispatchEvent`, and a trusted click on an attached anchor each delivered 300,000
+  bytes with a matching SHA-256 and started no browser download. A blob revoked before the click
+  fell back to the original download. After `remove()`, a normal click downloads again.
+- Not verified: Chrome 153 (the user's Playwright browser). The user's first run on 7.0.89 is the test.
+
+## Update
+From 7.0.88: `python3 ../AutoVideoAI-v7.0.89/tools/update_v788_to_v789.py . --apply`
+From 7.0.87: first apply `python3 ../AutoVideoAI-v7.0.88/tools/update_v787_to_v788.py . --apply`.
+Stop the dashboard first. Runtime data, jobs, profiles and outputs are never touched.
